1
00:00:56,699 --> 00:01:04,340
<v Tutor>Before we start, Jimmy and Kathy, thanks for coming in today to talk about your current research paper! 

2
00:01:04,340 --> 00:01:10,924
<v Tutor>Well, I will also give you some suggestions for your future presentation later.


3
00:01:10,924 --> 00:01:12,202
<v Jimmy>That’s great!


4
00:01:12,202 --> 00:01:19,821
<v Tutor>Okay, I have read the introductory chapter, and so far I like where you’re going with your research, you two.

5
00:01:19,821 --> 00:01:23,435
<v Kathy>Thanks! What did you think of the procedure section?

6
00:01:23,435 --> 00:01:30,579
<v Tutor>I haven’t gotten there yet. I will get to that and the results and discussion section in a bit.


7
00:01:30,579 --> 00:01:35,578
<v Jimmy>Oh. If you haven’t read the rest, are you just saying you like the introduction?


8
00:01:35,578 --> 00:01:48,419
<v Tutor>No - the layout is really well done. You have each section clearly marked and have the header and footer perfectly formatted, and your title page is right on the money.

9
00:01:48,419 --> 00:01:51,493
<v Tutor>A lot of students have trouble with that one.

10
00:01:51,493 --> 00:01:56,089
<v Jimmy>To be honest, we did refer a lot to the example we received in class.

11
00:01:56,089 --> 00:02:03,195
<v Tutor>That’s good to do for spacing and layout, as long as you’re not also copying the information. 

12
00:02:03,195 --> 00:02:09,513
<v Tutor>The background information is a little spare, though. You may want to add it.


13
00:02:09,513 --> 00:02:13,917
<v Kathy>You think so? I was more worried about whether I had enough data.


14
00:02:13,907 --> 00:02:23,904
<v Tutor>You definitely need more background information. I would think about finding some more online articles or doing more research in the campus library.

15
00:02:23,904 --> 00:02:32,147
<v Jimmy>That’s a good idea - we can go tomorrow. I find it too tough finding the subject matter in the online journal database.

16
00:02:32,147 --> 00:02:39,322
<v Tutor>I also like being able to flip through the physical journal as opposed to trying to scroll down on a computer.


17
00:02:39,322 --> 00:02:47,897
<v Kathy>Me too. Oh, I almost forgot. I’ve included all of my citations in the abstract, but could you help me with the bibliography? 

18
00:02:47,897 --> 00:02:51,722
<v Kathy>I should be using a bibliography, right? Not an appendix?

19
00:02:51,722 --> 00:03:03,584
<v Tutor>Sure, I can help with that. Yes - for this type of scientific research paper, list all sources that you cite in the body of your paper in a bibliography.

20
00:03:03,584 --> 00:03:10,008
<v Tutor>Go to the website I gave you last time to see the exact way to list each source.

21
00:03:10,008 --> 00:03:14,574
<v Kathy>Okay, thanks. I’ll do that. We still have a lot of things to fix up.

22
00:03:14,574 --> 00:03:22,324
<v Tutor>Yeah, but there’s a lot of good stuff here to work with. So enough about the paper, how is the presentation going?

23
00:03:22,324 --> 00:03:29,217
<v Kathy>Well, it’s alright. I am going to go try out the new presentation software while Jimmy’s working on the bibliography.


24
00:03:29,217 --> 00:03:36,509
<v Jimmy>Yeah, we are going to make an animation of an actual pump but still have a lot to learn about how to do that.


25
00:04:13,510 --> 00:04:21,809
<v Kathy>Who would have thought before we started this project that we would be able to recreate the motion of a pump? This stuff is just so interesting.

26
00:04:21,809 --> 00:04:23,367
<v Tutor>So glad to hear it!

27
00:04:23,367 --> 00:04:29,530
<v Jimmy>Yeah, I am glad I took engineering this semester. I would definitely like to keep up with it.


28
00:04:29,497 --> 00:04:37,388
<v Tutor>You know, there’s an organization called the Machine Engineering Society. You should look into joining it. 

29
00:04:37,388 --> 00:04:43,639
 <v Tutor>You would need to score well in your engineering class to qualify, but I think you can do it.

30
00:04:43,639 --> 00:04:46,747
<v Kathy>Hm, interesting. I will definitely check it out. 

31
00:04:46,747 --> 00:04:53,026
<v Kathy>I would really like to get in contact with some professionals in the engineering field to find out more. 

32
00:04:53,026 --> 00:04:56,028
<v Kathy>I don’t really know anyone in the field now, though.

33
00:04:56,028 --> 00:05:03,953
<v Jimmy>I think if you keep meeting people in your classes and professors you’ll be able to get in contact with some really helpful people.

34
00:05:03,953 --> 00:05:14,499
<v Tutor>Well said, Jimmy. If engineering pumps is something you both are specifically interested in, make sure you stay up to date on new developments.

35
00:05:14,499 --> 00:05:23,599
<v Tutor>In fact, you could visit the local water treatment facility periodically to see what new developments are going on.

36
00:05:23,599 --> 00:05:27,576
<v Kathy>Hm, that may be a good way to get some practical experience.


37
00:05:27,576 --> 00:05:33,897
<v Tutor>Well, I don’t think they would let you handle any equipment by just visiting the facility.

38
00:05:33,897 --> 00:05:42,242
<v Tutor>If you really want to get your hands dirty, so to speak, I would recommend instead seeking a summer internship.

39
00:05:42,242 --> 00:05:46,552
<v Kathy>Wow, you have so many helpful suggestions for getting a leg up. 

40
00:05:46,552 --> 00:05:50,394
<v Kathy>Now if only you could tell me how to get my work published!

41
00:05:50,394 --> 00:05:52,452
<v Jimmy>Haha, wouldn’t that be nice.

42
00:05:52,452 --> 00:05:59,604
<v Tutor>Well, honestly, all you really need to do is once you have a dissertation, present it.

43
00:05:59,604 --> 00:06:08,145
<v Tutor>Present it often and to many audiences, and once you get feedback, adjust it. I’ll get published one day.

44
00:06:08,145 --> 00:06:13,032
<v Kathy>Wow, this meeting has been truly inspiring. Thanks for your help!